VCDecTest - the vcdec library on the Raspberry Pi
================================================

vcdec is the library Reel and FFmpeg use to have H.264 decoded by the
VideoCore, through RISC OS's VCHIQ module. VCDecTest drives it as a player
would: each turn it sends access units until vcdec says the decoder's input
is full, then takes every picture that's ready. Each picture is copied into
the program's own memory and checked against a checksum FFmpeg made of the
same picture, matched by its time (pts). The VideoCore's pictures can differ
from FFmpeg's by 1 in a few values (rounding), so each is also compared by
block averages: "within rounding" is fine, anything more is a fault.

New in 0.3.9 (vcdec 0.4.9): 0.3.8's Start showed that giving the
decoder its output buffers at once is safe (every picture right, a seek
after the end and two 1080p decoders too, the first picture 1-5 cs after
the first access unit), so vcdec now always does. -W gives the old way,
for comparison; -G does nothing now. Start runs -W, then the default.

New in 0.3.8 (vcdec 0.4.8): Start. Reel Edit waits about 2 s for the
first picture at every clip it starts. vcdec gives the decoder its
output buffers only after the decoder says the picture size (a "format
change"), or after 200 cs, and with Reel Edit's clips that message
doesn't come by itself. That rule came from MMALDecode's first tests,
whose lost first picture turned out later to be something else. Start
times the first picture of each clip with vcdec's way, then with the
buffers given at once (-G), every picture checked. Each report now has
"Start:" lines. There's a new clip, flat (one colour, tiny pictures, as
Reel Edit's). Please restart, run Start and send ResultS. If a -G run
stops with "no reply", restart before anything else: that's an answer
too.

New in 0.3.7 (vcdec 0.4.7): 0.3.6's Multi showed three 1080p decoders
working at once (60 pictures a second together), and every decoder
getting direct output. But with 3 and 4 small decoders, one ended a
picture short. With direct output the VideoCore can hand back the end
of the stream before the last picture, and vcdec took that as the end.
Now a picture that comes after it is taken first, and vcdec waits a
moment at the end while pictures are still owed. Multi now lists any
missing pictures, and says when a picture came after the end. Please
restart, run Multi and send ResultMulti.

New in 0.3.6 (vcdec 0.4.6): one shared-memory service for the whole
program. With 0.3.5, Multi showed that direct output lets two 1080p
decoders work at once (before, the second waited for the first), but the
firmware refused a third program connection to its shared-memory
service, so a program's third and fourth decoders fell back to bulk
transfers. Now every decoder in a program shares one connection. Please
restart, run Multi and send ResultMulti: the small clip's 3 and 4
decoders should all say "60 direct". (A third 1080p decoder still has
no room in gpu_mem=128.)

New in 0.3.5 (vcdec 0.4.5): direct output. Until now the decoder made
each picture in its own memory (gpu_mem) and then sent it over by bulk
transfer, 3 MB a picture at 1080p. Now vcdec shares its buffers with the
VideoCore, as Linux does, and the decoder writes each picture straight
into them: no transfer, and no copy of the buffers in gpu_mem. SMEMProbe
showed this works from RISC OS. It's the default; -D turns it off (every
picture by bulk transfer, as before). Each report now ends with a
"Direct output" line saying how many pictures came that way.
Direct checks the three clips with it, holds hd's pictures to the end
and seeks after it, then times hd with it and without it, copied out
and held (ResultD). Please restart, run Direct, then Multi, and send
ResultD and ResultMulti: Multi shows whether a second 1080p decoder now
fits beside the first, now that the buffers aren't in gpu_mem.

New in 0.3.4 (vcdec 0.4.3): decoders give their memory back. A decoder
holds a share of the VideoCore's memory (one 1080p decoder fills
gpu_mem=128) until it's closed, and before, a program that stopped
without closing it left that memory taken until a restart. Now vcdec
closes every decoder as a program exits, and gives a decoder's memory
back as soon as its last picture has been taken (a seek creates it
again). Exit checks the first: three runs that stop after 30 pictures
without closing, then a whole one; all four should work (ResultX).
Multi now goes to 3 decoders at 1080p and 4 at 360p: 6 at 1080p
stopped the VideoCore answering. A second 1080p decoder now waits for
the first to finish, then decodes.
(A program stopped by Escape, or a crash, is still not covered: UnixLib
doesn't run exit handlers then. Restart after one.)

New in 0.3.3: Multi. An editor (Reel Edit) keeps several clips open at
once, each with its own VideoCore decoder. Multi opens 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6
decoders at once on the hd clip (then 4 and 8 on the small one), and
has each decode the whole clip, every picture checked, one picture from
each in turn. Each decoder holds its own memory in the VideoCore (from
gpu_mem) and about 20 MB of the Pi's for 1080p. The report says, for
each decoder, whether it opened, how many pictures came back right, and
its memory; then how fast they went together. A decoder the VideoCore
has no room for may be refused at once, or may take its input and send
nothing back: vcdec gives up on it after 5 seconds ("Nothing from the
decoder in 500 cs"), as a player would then fall back to the ARM.
Please restart, run Multi on its own and send ResultMulti. If a run
stops with "no reply", restart before running anything else.

New in 0.3.2 (vcdec 0.4.2): vcdec's pools never take the running
program's own page at &8000. RISC OS may offer pages a program is using
for contiguous memory and quietly copy the program elsewhere, and
ARMEABISupport, which recognises programs by the address of that page,
then loses the program (stacks left behind, and later the "EMT trap" in
another program). Each run now ends with a line saying where the page
at &8000 was at the start and at the end; "MOVED" is a failure. Please
restart first, run Test, Memory and ZeroCopy (send Result, ResultM
and ResultZ), then *ARMEABISupport_Info:
it should list no programs.

New in 0.3.1 (vcdec 0.4.1): zero-copy. VCDecTest 0.3 showed that at 82
pictures a second, copying each 1080p picture out of vcdec's buffer took
9 ms of its 12 while the VideoCore decoded and sent the next one (1.4 ms
on its own). Now a player can be handed vcdec's own buffer instead
(vcdec_receive_hold) and give it back when it has shown the picture
(vcdec_release): no copy. With -Z, VCDecTest takes pictures that way and
checks them where they are; -H n holds up to n at a time, as a player's
queue would; -R reads every byte of each once, as showing it would.

0.3 (vcdec 0.4) made a cacheable Physical Memory Pool and LDM 8 the
defaults (64 to 82 pictures a second), falling back to PCI memory when a
pool can't be had.

The clips (in clips, the same as before):
  small  640x360, 60 pictures, H.264 Main with B frames, a keyframe
         every 20
  odd    426x240, 30 pictures, H.264 Baseline (a width that isn't a
         multiple of 32, to check the cropping)
  hd     1920x1080, 150 pictures, H.264 High, level 4.1, B frames
  flat   1920x1080, 100 pictures of one colour, H.264 High, no B
         frames (0.3.8)

Running it (please restart first, then send back ResultS):
  Start    New: how long the first picture takes (see above). Writes
           ResultS.
  Direct   New: direct output, checked and timed (see above). Writes
           ResultD.
  Exit     Programs that stop without closing (see above). Writes
           ResultX.
  Multi    Several decoders open at once (see above). Writes
           ResultMulti.
  ZeroCopy The three clips taken without copying, every picture
           checked where it is; then hd holding 4 at a time, to the end,
           a seek after it, and closing with pictures still held. Writes
           ResultZ. Run this first.
  Speed    hd without checks - vcdec's copy, then zero-copy:
           held and released, held and read once, with 4 buffers, and 4
           held at a time in 6. Writes ResultT.
  Test     The three clips with vcdec's defaults (a cacheable pool,
           LDM 8, copied out), every picture checked. Writes Result.
  Memory   The three clips checked in PCI memory, copied by LDM 8 (what
           vcdec falls back to). Writes ResultM.
  SeekEnd  The small and hd clips, each to the end, then a flush (the
           decoder created again) and on from the last keyframe. Writes
           ResultEnd.
  Sync     As Test, each bulk transfer waited for. Writes ResultSync.
  Seek     The small clip; a flush after 10 pictures, then on from the
           keyframe at picture 40. Writes ResultSeek.
  Verbose  The small clip with every message (ResultV).

  Each report says how much memory the VideoCore has (gpu_mem in
  CONFIG/TXT); 1080p needs gpu_mem=128 or more, and where each pool's
  pages are.

If a run stops part way (the report says "no reply"), the VideoCore's
decoder can stay stuck until the machine is restarted: restart before
running anything else. If the machine locks up (it shouldn't), switch it
off and on again.

Needs: RISC OS 5 on a Raspberry Pi, with the VCHIQ module (in the ROM);
the pools need RISC OS 5.23 or later (on older ones, PCI memory as before).
Part of riscos-reelhwaccel; GPL version 2. The clips are made
from FFmpeg's testsrc2 pattern.
